# ---------------- Abstract ----------------
heading('Abstract')
para('**Motivation:** Variant callsets and genome annotation are increasingly stored as Parquet files inside open table formats such as Delta Lake and Apache Iceberg, and queried by region directly on cloud object storage. A region query is an interval overlap test, and its lower bound can only be checked against the largest end coordinate in each row group. A few long records, such as structural variants or gene bodies, inflate that statistic for every row stored beside them. Interval indexes solve the same problem in memory by storing long intervals apart, and the same idea has been proposed for lakehouse storage. Whether it pays on real data, and on object storage where every request has a fixed cost, has not been measured.', indent=False)
para('**Results:** We measured separation of long records and row group sizing on seven tables built from GENCODE, ClinVar, RepeatMasker, 1000 Genomes, gnomAD v4.1 and the Database of Genomic Variants, checking every layout row for row against an unpruned scan. On annotation and population tables, whose interval lengths are continuous, separation never reduced bytes read, and on structural variant data it made queries take 71 to 96% longer. On the one real table with a bimodal length distribution, the 1000 Genomes phase 3 chr22 callset, separation reduced bytes by up to 41%, yet on a remote store it was 3.5 to 4.6% slower than a sorted layout with a tuned row group size, because the long records form a row group that every query must request. Choosing the row group size with a model of requests and bytes did pay: 66% and 73% less query time in Iceberg and Delta tables on a gnomAD v4.1 window, 14% against the DuckDB default on chr22, and 7 to 10% on DGV, where the model selected the measured optimum in all six workloads. We also calibrate the staleness of a materialized allele frequency: after 10% cohort growth, 5.6% of variants with frequency at least 0.1% sit in the wrong curation class.', indent=False)
para('**Availability and implementation:** SWaPO is open source under the MIT licence at [repository URL]. All inputs are public and identified by SHA256 in the repository; every figure is regenerated from the stored results.', indent=False)
para('**Contact:** [corresponding author email]', indent=False)
para('**Supplementary information:** Supplementary data are available at *Bioinformatics* online.', indent=False)

# ---------------- 1 Introduction ----------------
heading('1 Introduction')
para('Most questions asked of a genomic data platform name a region. A variant browser, a clinical interpretation pipeline, an annotation join and a gene burden test each reduce to the same predicate over half open intervals: a record overlaps the query when its start is before the query end and its end is after the query start. Tools such as tabix (Li, 2011) and BEDTools (Quinlan and Hall, 2010) answer this over indexed files, and in memory structures such as the nested containment list (Alekseyenko and Lee, 2007) and the augmented interval list (Feng *et al.*, 2019) answer it quickly for large interval sets.', indent=False)
para('Increasingly the same data live in a lakehouse (Armbrust *et al.*, 2021): Parquet files (Apache Software Foundation, 2024a), organised as a Delta Lake (Armbrust *et al.*, 2020) or Apache Iceberg (Apache Software Foundation, 2024b) table, read by an engine such as DuckDB (Raasveldt and Mühleisen, 2019) or Spark directly from object storage. Project Glow (Project Glow, 2024), Hail (Hail Team, 2024), ADAM (Massie *et al.*, 2013) and TileDB (Papadopoulos *et al.*, 2016) all build on this pattern or its relatives. In a Parquet file, rows are grouped into row groups, and the footer records the minimum and maximum of each column for each row group. An engine skips a row group when those statistics prove that no row can match (Moerkotte, 1998; Sun *et al.*, 2014).')
para('For an overlap query this pruning is asymmetric. Sorting by start makes the minimum start of each row group useful for the upper bound of the query. The lower bound can only be tested against the maximum end, and a maximum is destroyed by one outlier. Genomic interval lengths span six orders of magnitude: a table that holds single nucleotide variants beside a two megabase deletion, or exons beside gene bodies, gives the row group containing the long record a reach far beyond its own rows, and every query in that reach must read it (Figure 1A). The interval index literature has a known remedy, which is to store the long, containing intervals apart so they cannot distort the dense majority (Alekseyenko and Lee, 2007; Feng *et al.*, 2019). Applied to storage, this gives a layout in which long records form their own row groups (Figure 1B).')
figure('fig1_mechanism', 6.3, 'Fig. 1. The pruning problem and the proposed remedy. (A) Rows sorted by start. A long record in RG 2 raises its maximum end, so a query over RG 3 must also read RG 2. (B) Long records stored as their own row group. The short row groups are tight again, but the long row group spans the contig and is read by every query, at the cost of one additional request on object storage.')
para('We set out to build and validate this remedy, and found that it does not pay in the setting that matters. This paper reports what does decide the cost of a region query on object storage, measured on real public data. Our contributions are:')
for item in (
    '1. A two condition account of when separating long records helps. It can reduce bytes only when the length distribution is bimodal, which we measure with a one pass statistic, the ratio of the 99.99th to the 99th percentile of record span. It can reduce time only when the bytes it saves outweigh the request it adds, which depends on the latency and bandwidth of the store.',
    '2. Measurements on seven tables from six public sources, including a 2.33 Mb window of gnomAD v4.1 at 279 records per kilobase, showing that annotation, clinical, repeat, population and structural variant tables fail the first condition, and that the one bimodal callset we found fails the second once row group size is tuned.',
    '3. A row group size advisor that predicts requests and bytes from Parquet footers, validated against logged HTTP requests, and measured on Delta Lake and Iceberg tables with planner agreement and rollback checked.',
    '4. A calibration of how quickly a materialized allele frequency becomes wrong as a cohort grows, from the real 1000 Genomes phase 1 to phase 3 expansion.',
    '5. An account of how a preliminary evaluation of our own overstated the benefit of separation, and what to check to avoid the same errors.'):
    para(item, indent=False)
para('We do not claim novelty for workload driven physical design. Cost based design advisors date to AutoAdmin (Chaudhuri and Narasayya, 1997, 1998), and automatic selection of clustering keys from scan telemetry is in production for lakehouse tables (Nakandala *et al.*, 2026). Our question is narrower and is not addressed by clustering: for genomic tables, sorting by start is already the right clustering, and what remains is how the interval length distribution and the row group size interact with the cost of reading from object storage.')

# ---------------- 2 Methods ----------------
heading('2 Methods')
heading('2.1 Region queries and row group statistics', 2)
para('All coordinates are converted once, at the readers, to zero based half open intervals. A query [*q*_{s}, *q*_{e}) overlaps a record [*s*, *e*) when *s* < *q*_{e} and *q*_{s} < *e*. A row group with minimum start *S*_{min} and maximum end *E*_{max} can be skipped unless *S*_{min} < *q*_{e} and *q*_{s} < *E*_{max}. We call *E*_{max} − *S*_{min} the reach of a row group and the ratio of reach to the range of starts it holds its tightness. A tightness of one means no row extends past the row group that holds it.'.replace('−', 'minus'), indent=False)
heading('2.2 Cost of a region query on object storage', 2)
para('We read the request pattern of DuckDB 1.x over HTTP from a server that logs every request, including HEAD requests. For each file the reader issues a HEAD request, reads a 16 kB tail, and reads the footer separately when it is larger than the tail. For each row group that survives pruning it issues one coalesced range request for the needed columns, split at 2 MiB. The footer grows by roughly 1 kB per row group, which is what makes very small row groups expensive. A query that issues *R* requests and transfers *B* bytes takes')
equation('*T*  =  *R* · λ  +  *B* / β', 1)
para('where λ is the latency of one request and β the bandwidth of one stream. Because *R* and *B* do not depend on the store, a single run can be scored exactly at any store profile. We measured λ = 170 ms and β = 5.2 MB/s from a workstation to Google Cloud Storage and use this as the remote profile; we also report a same region cloud profile (30 ms, 90 MB/s) and a local object store (1 ms, 500 MB/s), consistent with the ranges reported by Durner *et al.* (2023). The product λβ, the bytes that cost as much as one request, is 884 kB, 2.7 MB and 500 kB for the three profiles.', indent=False)
heading('2.3 Separating long records', 2)
para('The separated layout writes records with span at least a threshold τ to their own row groups, in the same file or in a separate partition, and leaves the rest sorted by start. Because the long row groups have bounds that cover most of the contig, nearly every query reads them. Separation therefore trades an additional request per query, and the bytes of the long row group, against the row groups that the short layout no longer has to read. It can only win when a threshold exists that moves very few rows and removes almost all of the reach, which requires a gap in the length distribution. We measure this with the separability')
equation('*S*  =  *p*_{99.99} / *p*_{99}', 2)
para('of record span, computed in one pass. Our threshold search evaluates candidate thresholds at span quantiles without writing data, because row group boundaries are a deterministic function of the sort order, and keeps the unseparated layout unless a candidate beats it outright.', indent=False)
heading('2.4 Choosing the row group size', 2)
para('For each candidate size from 2,048 to 1,048,576 rows the advisor computes, from the sorted table, the row group boundaries, their start and end bounds and, from a paired sample of contiguous windows written at every candidate size, the bytes per row group and the footer size. It then predicts *R* and *B* for the workload with the request pattern of Section 2.2 and chooses the size that minimises Equation (1). Measuring every candidate at the same sample offsets matters: with independent samples the difference between neighbouring sizes carries the error of both, which near a flat optimum is large enough to reverse the ranking. The workload is supplied as a list of query intervals, typically parsed from a query log; a log provides the shape of the workload, and the query volume is a separate input when the decision is whether to rewrite.', indent=False)
heading('2.5 Staleness of materialized allele frequencies', 2)
para('A precomputed cohort allele frequency is a dated claim, not a cache: when samples are added it becomes wrong rather than slow. We calibrated how wrong using the real expansion of 1000 Genomes from phase 1 (1000 Genomes Project Consortium, 2012) to phase 3 (1000 Genomes Project Consortium, 2015). Using the phase 3 genotypes for both, we computed every chr21 frequency on the 1,033 phase 1 individuals present in phase 3, added the remaining 1,471 individuals one population at a time in 50 random orders, and counted variants whose class changed. Classes follow the thresholds used in variant curation (Richards *et al.*, 2015; Whiffin *et al.*, 2017): absent, below 0.1%, 0.1 to 1%, 1 to 5% (the BS1 range) and at least 5% (BA1).', indent=False)
heading('2.6 Data', 2)
para('All inputs are public files downloaded unmodified, identified by SHA256 (Table 1). GENCODE v44 (Frankish *et al.*, 2023), ClinVar (Landrum *et al.*, 2018) and UCSC RepeatMasker (Smit *et al.*, 2015) provide annotation, clinical variants and repeats. The 1000 Genomes integrated structural variant map (Sudmant *et al.*, 2015) and phase 3 callsets (1000 Genomes Project Consortium, 2015) provide population variants. A 2.33 Mb window of the gnomAD v4.1 chr21 genomes sites file (Gudmundsson *et al.*, 2025), chr21:16,918,425 to 19,246,885, was read from the 7.2 GB remote file with BGZF range requests, which are valid because each BGZF block is an independent gzip member. The DGV merged track (MacDonald *et al.*, 2014) provides structural variants on GRCh38. Every table records its assembly per source. The chr21 density stress table combines GRCh38 annotation with the GRCh37 phase 3 callset; its coordinates cannot be joined biologically and we use it only to test layout behaviour at high density, alongside a GRCh38 only control.', indent=False)
table('Table 1. Tables measured. Separability is the ratio of the 99.99th to the 99th percentile of record span.',
      ['Table', 'Assembly', 'Rows', 'Records per kb', 'Separability'],
      [['chr1 annotation (GENCODE, ClinVar, RepeatMasker, 1000G SV)', 'GRCh38^{a}', '1,046,208', '4.2', '75'],
       ['chr1 control (GENCODE, ClinVar, RepeatMasker)', 'GRCh38', '1,041,537', '4.2', '88'],
       ['chr21 density stress table (adds 1000G phase 3)', 'mixed', '1,245,415', '26.7', '407'],
       ['gnomAD v4.1 chr21 window with GENCODE', 'GRCh38', '650,304', '279', '129'],
       ['DGV merged structural variants, all contigs', 'GRCh38', '388,687', 'n/a', '7.5'],
       ['1000 Genomes phase 3 chr22 callset (SNV, indel, SV)', 'GRCh37', '1,105,249', '31.4', '88,153'],
       ['Simulator, long fraction 0.004', 'n/a', '300,000', 'n/a', '39,701']],
      [7.6, 2.0, 2.2, 2.3, 2.3],
      note='^{a}The 4,671 structural variant rows (0.45%) are GRCh37; the GRCh38 only control gives the same result.')
heading('2.7 Workloads and correctness', 2)
para('Queries come from five real sets: protein coding gene bodies from GENCODE, ClinVar pathogenic and likely pathogenic variants, GWAS Catalog lead variants with 250 kb on each side (Sollis *et al.*, 2023), exons of the ACMG SF v3.2 genes (Miller *et al.*, 2023) and UCSC cytogenetic bands. On the GRCh37 chr22 callset we avoid cross assembly coordinates entirely: query centres are drawn from the table’s own records, which weights them by density, with widths of 1 bp (variant), GENCODE gene lengths (gene) and 500 kb (association), 1,000 queries each. Every layout is checked against an unpruned count for a sample of its queries, and the pruning code is tested against brute force on randomised inputs, including multi contig row groups, zero width queries and boundaries that touch. No layout reported here returned a wrong count.', indent=False)

# ---------------- 3 Results ----------------
heading('3 Results')
heading('3.1 Real interval lengths are continuous', 2)
para('Real genomic sources produce records at every scale. GENCODE spans move smoothly from 138 bp at the median through 88 kb at the 99th percentile to 1.55 Mb at the maximum, because exons, introns, transcripts, genes and gene clusters are all present. ClinVar is almost all single bases with a short tail; RepeatMasker sits between 50 bp and 10 kb; structural variant maps are broad by construction. Combined into a platform table, as a lakehouse stores them, these give separability between 75 and 407 (Table 1). DGV, which holds only structural variants, has separability 7.5. Our simulator, which like many synthetic benchmarks drew a majority of single base records and a small log uniform tail of long ones, has separability 39,701: its 99th percentile is 46 bp and its 99.99th percentile 1.85 Mb.', indent=False)
heading('3.2 Separation does not reduce bytes on continuous tables', 2)
para('On the chr1 annotation table, at every row group size from 8,000 rows down to 30, the threshold search declined to separate; at 250 rows per block, forcing any candidate threshold increased the expected blocks read by 28 to 50%. The decline was correct: measured bytes were identical with and without separation at all eight sizes, even though tightness rose from 1.05 to 26.35 as row groups narrowed. The same held for the GRCh38 only control, for the chr21 density stress table at 26.7 records per kilobase, and for the gnomAD window at 279 records per kilobase, five sizes each. On the stress table tightness reached 2.11 and 2.32, where the simulator already gained 15%; tightness alone therefore does not predict a gain. Across 32 configurations, including the synthetic ones, the search proposed separation exactly when separation measurably reduced bytes: 4 correct proposals and 28 correct refusals (Figure 2).', indent=False)
figure('fig2_separability', 3.4, 'Fig. 2. Separation only reduces bytes on bimodal length distributions. Best reduction in bytes read over all thresholds and row group sizes tested, against separability. The four annotation and population tables and DGV gain nothing or lose; the simulator and the bimodal 1000 Genomes chr22 callset gain.')
para('Density does not change this. Density in genomic tables comes from short records, because more samples add more single nucleotide variants, never longer genes. Adding short records narrows row groups, which increases the harm of long records, but also dilutes the long records across more row groups. Measured separability tracks the composition of a table, not its density: 75 at 4.2 records per kilobase, 407 at 26.7 and 129 at 279.')
heading('3.3 Structural variant data: separation is slower', 2)
para('On DGV, separation into a long partition at the 90th, 99th and 99.9th percentile of span (75 kb, 444 kb and 1.56 Mb) took 71 to 96% longer at the remote profile, at both 8,192 and 122,880 rows per row group, across 500 mixed queries. Bytes barely changed, but each query now opened two files, so requests roughly doubled from 3.0 to between 5.4 and 6.0. With separability 7.5 there is no threshold that moves few rows and removes most of the reach.', indent=False)
heading('3.4 A bimodal callset: bytes fall, time does not', 2)
para('The 1000 Genomes phase 3 chr22 callset holds 1,105,249 records in one VCF: single nucleotide variants, short indels and 2,347 structural variants of at least 500 bp, the longest a 3.47 Mb duplication. Its 99th percentile span is 4 bp and its 99.99th 353 kb, giving separability 88,153, higher than our simulator. This is the regime where separation should work, and in bytes it does (Table 2, Figure 3A). Writing the long records as their own row groups in the same file reduced bytes read per gene query by 15 to 39% at every row group size from 2,048 to 65,536 rows, and by up to 41% for variant queries.', indent=False)
table('Table 2. Separation on the 1000 Genomes chr22 callset, 1,000 gene queries, threshold 500 bp. Time is Equation (1) at the remote profile (170 ms, 5.2 MB/s). Rows are checked against an unpruned count in every layout.',
      ['Rows per row group', 'Row groups read, sorted', 'Row groups read, separated', 'Requests, sorted', 'Requests, separated', 'Bytes saved', 'Time, sorted (ms)', 'Time, separated (ms)'],
      [['2,048', '4.30', '2.47', '7.30', '5.47', '15%', '1,314', '992'],
       ['8,192', '2.72', '2.12', '5.72', '5.12', '39%', '1,044', '914'],
       ['16,384', '2.02', '2.06', '5.02', '5.06', '38%', '945', '918'],
       ['32,768', '1.61', '2.03', '4.61', '5.03', '31%', '922', '951'],
       ['50,000', '1.49', '2.02', '3.48', '4.01', '29%', '**784**', '819'],
       ['65,536', '1.36', '2.01', '3.36', '4.01', '24%', '801', '858'],
       ['122,880 (DuckDB default)', '1.17', '2.01', '3.17', '4.01', '13%', '914', '1,009'],
       ['1,048,576 (pyarrow default)', '1.02', '2.00', '7.87', '8.78', '1%', '3,401', '3,534']],
      [3.3, 1.9, 2.1, 1.7, 1.9, 1.5, 1.8, 2.0])
para('Time is a different matter. The long row group overlaps almost every query, so the separated layout reads two row groups per query at every size, one more request than a sorted layout with large row groups. Separation only reduces requests where reach pollution makes the sorted layout read more than two row groups, which happens below about 16,384 rows. At 50,000 rows it saves 0.29 MB per query, a third of the 884 kB that one request costs at the remote profile, and is 4.4% slower. The decisive comparison is between each layout at its own best size (Figure 3B). At the remote profile the sorted layout at 50,000 rows took 784 ms per gene query and the best separated layout 819 ms, 4.4% slower; for variant and association queries the separated layout was 3.5% and 4.6% slower. At the same region profile it was 12.5 to 13.8% slower. Only on a local object store, where a request costs 1 ms, did it gain, by 0.3 to 1.8%. At both cloud profiles, every small row group size at which separation wins is slower than the tuned sorted layout.', indent=False)
figure('fig3_callset_split', 6.5, 'Fig. 3. Separation on the bimodal 1000 Genomes chr22 callset. (A) Saving from separation at each row group size, in bytes (dashed) and in time at three store profiles. Bytes always fall; time only falls at small row groups. (B) Mean time per gene query at the remote profile. The best sorted layout (50,000 rows) is faster than the best separated layout.')
heading('3.5 Row group size decides the cost', 2)
para('Every comparison above contains a larger effect than separation: the row group size. On chr22 the best sorted size was 14% faster than the DuckDB default of 122,880 rows for gene and variant queries and 10% for association queries, and 77% faster than the default of 1,048,576 rows used by pyarrow, pyiceberg and delta rs, which for this table writes a single row group for almost every row. The cost curve is U shaped: smaller row groups are pruned more precisely, but each surviving row group costs a request and every row group adds about 1 kB of footer.', indent=False)
para('We validated the advisor against logged requests on DGV, over five query sets and a mixed set of 500 queries at ten sizes each. Predicted requests equalled logged requests in all 60 configurations, and predicted bytes were within 0.9% above and 11.7% below the measured bytes. In all six workloads the advisor chose the size that was fastest when measured (2,048 or 4,096 rows), 7 to 10% faster than the default (Figure 4A). DGV contigs hold about 16,000 rows each, so every size above 32,768 rows already gives one row group per contig, which limits the available gain.', indent=False)
para('On the gnomAD v4.1 window, written by the native Delta Lake and Iceberg writers as one row group of 649,879 rows, the advisor recommended 65,536 rows and predicted a 62.8% saving. After rewriting through the native writers, 86 real queries that fall within the window took 73.3% less time on Delta Lake (3,996 to 1,067 ms) and 66.3% less on Iceberg (3,164 to 1,066 ms), with time computed from the logged requests and bytes (Figure 4B). The files planned from our statistics agreed with the native planners for 86 of 86 queries before and after, every query returned the correct rows, and reading the previous table version returned all 649,879 rows.', indent=False)
figure('fig4_rowgroups', 6.5, 'Fig. 4. Row group sizing. (A) DGV, 500 mixed queries at the remote profile: time measured from logged requests and bytes against time predicted from footers, at ten row group sizes. (B) gnomAD v4.1 window, 86 queries, Delta Lake and Apache Iceberg tables as written by default and after the recommended rewrite.')
heading('3.6 Materialized allele frequencies go stale quickly', 2)
para('Unlike a layout change, a materialized frequency changes the answer. After 10% cohort growth, a median of 5.6% (10th to 90th percentile over arrival orders, 4.6 to 9.8%) of variants with frequency at least 0.1% were in the wrong class (Figure 5). The real phase 1 to phase 3 expansion, 142% growth, moved 19.1% of them, including 6,050 variants that crossed the BA1 threshold and 18,137 that crossed BS1, and 400,274 variants absent from the phase 1 cohort appeared. The advisor uses this curve to charge a materialization for expected wrong answers in proportion to the rate at which samples arrive, so the same projection is accepted on a frozen release and rejected on a growing cohort.', indent=False)
figure('fig5_staleness', 3.4, 'Fig. 5. Share of chr21 variants with allele frequency at least 0.1% whose curation class is wrong after the cohort grows, over 50 random population arrival orders, with the real 1000 Genomes phase 1 to phase 3 expansion.')
heading('3.7 How an earlier evaluation overstated separation', 2)
para('A preliminary internal evaluation of ours reported that separation made cold object storage queries 1.5 to 1.7 times faster and read 47% fewer bytes on the chr22 callset. Repeating it with the protocol above traced the difference to five causes, each of which we think is common enough to state. First, it used three loci, all on 22q11.2, where the largest structural variant calls in the file end (deletions of 2.0 and 2.1 Mb ending at 21,068,174); there the sorted layout read two row groups for every query, against 1.49 on average over 1,000 density weighted queries, and the byte saving was 47% against 29%. Second, the store was a local object store, where a request costs about 1 ms; at that profile we model a gain of 0.8%, so the measured speedup was mostly timing variance over three queries. Third, the query coordinates were GRCh38 while the callset is GRCh37. Fourth, a whole genome result was built by copying one chromosome under 22 names. Fifth, cohort genotype matrices were simulated. None of these errors produced a wrong row; all of them produced a wrong conclusion.', indent=False)

# ---------------- 4 Discussion ----------------
heading('4 Discussion')
para('Separating long intervals is the right idea for an in memory index, where the cost of visiting a second list is a few pointer reads. On object storage the second list is a row group that every query has to request, and a request costs as much as hundreds of kilobytes of transfer. The benefit therefore needs two conditions: a length distribution with a gap, so that separation can remove reach without moving many rows, and saved bytes larger than the bandwidth delay product of the store. Of seven real and simulated tables, only the simulator met both at practical row group sizes. Annotation, clinical, repeat, population and structural variant tables fail the first condition, and the one bimodal callset fails the second once the row group size is chosen well.', indent=False)
para('This has a practical reading. Before adopting any interval aware layout, compute the separability of the table; below roughly a thousand, separation is unlikely to reduce bytes at all. Then choose the row group size for the store and the workload, which on our measurements was worth 7 to 77% depending on how far the default was from the optimum, needs no change to the file format or the reader, and is respected by every engine. Only then compare a separated layout at its own best size against the sorted layout at its best size. Comparing both at an untuned default, or reporting bytes without requests, will overstate separation, because the default is usually the regime where reach pollution is worst.', indent=False)
para('Automatic clustering services choose which columns to cluster on (Nakandala *et al.*, 2026). For genomic intervals that choice is already settled, and our results suggest the more useful automation is choosing the row group size from the query log and the measured store profile, and refusing rewrites that do not pay. A decision procedure for a rewrite of a large table is mostly valuable for its refusals: 28 of our 32 block level decisions and every separation decision on real tables were correct refusals.', indent=False)
heading('4.1 Limitations', 2)
para('Times are computed from logged or predicted requests and bytes with Equation (1), not from wall clock time; the model ignores parallel requests, which some readers issue, and compute cost. We measured one engine, DuckDB, whose request pattern we logged; other readers coalesce differently. Our largest single table has 1.25 million rows and our gnomAD data is a contiguous 2.33 Mb window, not the whole genome, so the absolute savings at full scale will differ, although the mechanisms depend on row group geometry rather than table size. We found one public table with a bimodal length distribution; a somatic callset with many large rearrangements could be another, and Figure 3 shows how to test it. We did not compare against tabix, TileDB VCF or GenomicsDB, which solve the same queries with different storage and are not lakehouse formats.', indent=False)

# ---------------- 5 Conclusion ----------------
heading('5 Conclusion')
para('For genomic region queries over Parquet on cloud object storage, the row group size, chosen for the workload and the store, decided the cost in every case we measured, while separating long intervals helped only in a regime that tuning removes. A table’s separability predicts in one pass whether separation can help at all, and the bandwidth delay product of the store decides whether bytes saved are worth a request. We release the advisor, the measurements and every input checksum so that these conclusions can be tested on other tables.', indent=False)

